<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Firas al-Hamdani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">932–<a href="/years/968">968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Firas al-Hamdani was an Arab poet and warrior, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>, the Hamdanid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. He served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manbij">Manbij</a> and fought in the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, during which he was captured and spent several years as a prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His poems written in captivity, the Rumiyyat, are among the most admired of classical Arabic poetry. He was killed in 968 in a dynastic dispute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-firas-al-hamdani.jpg" alt="Abu Firas al-Hamdani" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist, 1963 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abou_Feras_al-Hamdani_(Syrian_Post,_1963).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Firas, born al-Harith ibn Abi'l-Ala Sa'id, was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where his father, a son of the Hamdanid family's founder Hamdan ibn Hamdun, held a distinguished place at the court of the Abbasid caliph al-Muqtadir. His mother was a Byzantine Greek slave concubine, a descent for which his Hamdanid relatives later taunted him. His father was killed in 935 in a dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> with his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a>, and his mother fled to Nasir al-Dawla's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>. Raised at Sayf al-Dawla's court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, among the philosopher al-Farabi, the preacher Ibn Nubata and the poet al-Mutanabbi, he was tutored by the grammarian Ibn Khalawayh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At 16 he was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manbij">Manbij</a>, near the Byzantine frontier, and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> too. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> tribes and joined his cousin's raids into Byzantine territory, and in 952 defeated a Byzantine force that tried to stop the refortification of Ra'ban and Mar'ash. The Arabic sources date his capture differently; most place it in 962, when he rode out with 70 men against a raid near Manbij led by the Byzantine general Theodore Parsakoutenos.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a> would not ransom him alone while thousands of other captives remained, and he was freed only in a general prisoner exchange at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> in 966. Named governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, he quarrelled after Sayf al-Dawla's death with the 15-year-old heir Abu'l-Ma'ali, his own sister's son, revolted, and was defeated and killed by the general Karghawayh on 4 April 968.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His early poems include qasidas praising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a>, the 225-line Hamdaniyyah the best known, shorter courtly poems on love, wine, hunting and friendship, and pro-Shia poems attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. Encouraged by Ibn Khalawayh, a bitter opponent of al-Mutanabbi, he set himself up as the older poet's rival, though his work shows his influence. H. A. R. Gibb praised the qasidas for their sincerity, directness and natural vigour.</p><p>The scholar Sahib ibn Abbad wrote that poetry began with a king, Imru al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>, and ended with a king, Abu Firas. Ibn Khalawayh edited his collected works after his death with a commentary largely written by the poet himself, though other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> versions with significant variations also survived.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Firas al-Hamdani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His court gathered the poet al-Mutanabbi, his cousin the poet <b>Abu Firas</b>, and the philosopher al-Farabi.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in Sadad, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=955" aria-label="Mosul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Firas al-Hamdani"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5897 4407h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5897 4407h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.34,43.13&amp;year=955">What was here in 955 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
